WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.633
The encoder.

2
00:00:01.933 --> 00:00:04.635
This is the half of the transformer that reads.

3
00:00:04.635 --> 00:00:07.567
It can't write a single word, and that's by design.

4
00:00:08.767 --> 00:00:11.467
An encoder is a stack of identical layers.

5
00:00:11.767 --> 00:00:13.367
Tokens come in at the bottom.

6
00:00:13.667 --> 00:00:18.600
Each layer starts with self-attention, where every token attends to every other token.

7
00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:23.667
Then a feed-forward network, wrapped in residual connections and normalization.

8
00:00:23.967 --> 00:00:27.900
After N rounds, out comes one contextual vector per token.

9
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.433
Look at who can see whom.

10
00:00:30.733 --> 00:00:32.005
Every cell is filled.

11
00:00:32.005 --> 00:00:36.000
The word dock attends to words on its left and right, all at once.

12
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.267
Because neighbors shape each vector, the same word gets a different vector in every sentence.

13
00:00:42.567 --> 00:00:46.900
Dock in a harbor sentence and dock in a spacecraft sentence land far apart.

14
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:48.867
Word2Vec could never do this.

15
00:00:50.067 --> 00:00:51.233
Here it is with BERT.

16
00:00:51.533 --> 00:00:52.733
We load an encoder.

17
00:00:53.033 --> 00:00:57.933
A helper returns the final vector for the word dock in each of two sentences.

18
00:00:58.233 --> 00:01:00.080
The cosine is well below one.

19
00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:01.800
Same word, different sense.

20
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.667
So what are encoders used for?

21
00:01:04.967 --> 00:01:07.500
Classification, with a small head on top.

22
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.600
Embeddings for semantic search.

23
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:11.791
Token labeling and reranking.

24
00:01:11.791 --> 00:01:14.856
All of these just need a good vector per token.

25
00:01:14.856 --> 00:01:17.333
An encoder has no notion of next word.

26
00:01:18.533 --> 00:01:20.600
Your embedding model is an encoder.

27
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:26.100
Every chunk goes in, and mean pooling produces one seven sixty eight dimensional vector.

28
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.533
That vector sits in your vector database.

29
00:01:28.833 --> 00:01:34.167
So when retrieval confuses docking port with port thruster, you know which component to swap.

30
00:01:35.367 --> 00:01:36.110
Let's recap.

31
00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:39.333
An encoder stacks attention and feed-forward layers.

32
00:01:39.633 --> 00:01:42.867
It's bidirectional: every token sees every token.

33
00:01:43.167 --> 00:01:45.900
It outputs one contextual vector per token.

34
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.757
It's for understanding, not generating.

35
00:01:48.757 --> 00:01:50.133
Next up: the decoder.
